course: technology
lesson: 23
title: Airports, the black box, helicopters and drones
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: An airport is like a huge machine for sorting people, bags and planes. Each part has its own job: scanners check bags, gates check passports, hidden belts carry suitcases, and controllers in a tall tower keep every plane apart.
explain: Your suitcase disappears behind a rubber curtain at check-in with a paper tag on it. A barcode on that tag is scanned again and again as the bag rides kilometres of hidden belts to the right plane.
explain: Not everything that flies has fixed wings. A helicopter's spinning blades are long thin wings going round in a circle, a drone does the same with four or more small rotors, and a hot-air balloon simply floats because hot air is lighter than cold air.
explain: If something ever goes wrong in the air, two tough recorders called the black box help investigators find out why, so that it never happens again.
fact: At security, bags pass through an X-ray machine that shows what is inside, and people walk through a metal detector or body scanner.
fact: Never joke about bombs or weapons at an airport; staff must treat every such word as real. Liquids are limited because some explosives can be liquid.
fact: Air traffic controllers in towers and control centres use radar (radio waves bounced off planes to find where they are) and radio to keep planes apart and tell them when to take off and land; thousands of planes are in the sky at the same time.
fact: Bags are tagged with barcodes and sent along kilometres of hidden belts to the right plane; nobody should ever climb on the baggage carousel.
fact: The black box is really two tough recorders, one for flight data and one for cockpit sounds, and they are painted bright orange so they can be found.
fact: Runways have lights to guide pilots in the dark and fog, and their big painted numbers show the compass direction divided by ten, so runway 27 points roughly west, to 270 degrees; on a compass north is 0 or 360 degrees, east 90, south 180 and west 270.
fact: Planes keep most of their fuel inside their wings, filled from underground pipes or tanker lorries.
fact: A helicopter's rotor blades are long thin wings that spin; the small tail rotor stops the body spinning the other way.
fact: Nobody goes near a helicopter until the crew says so, because its blades spin too fast to see; air ambulances can land in fields and on hospital roofs.
fact: A hot-air balloon rises because a burner heats the air inside it and hot air is lighter than the cold air outside; the first people flew in one in Paris in 1783.
fact: A balloon pilot cannot steer left or right; they go up or down to find winds blowing in different directions.
fact: A drone flies with four or more small rotors spinning at different speeds, and some hold their place in the air using GPS.
fact: Drones must never be flown near airports, crowds or power lines; near an airport it is against the law and can put planes in danger, and in the UK many drones must be registered.
fact: A passport gate's camera compares your face with the photo stored on a tiny chip in your passport.
exercise: What does the X-ray machine at airport security do? | It shows what is inside your bag without opening it | It weighs your bag to work out how much your ticket costs | It cleans your bag from germs | It warms your bag for the cold hold
exercise: Why are big bottles of liquid not allowed through airport security? | Some explosives can be liquid, so liquids are limited | Water makes the plane too heavy to fly | Liquids freeze solid and burst open in the cold cabin at night | Airports want you to buy their own drinks
exercise: How do planes avoid crashing into each other? | Air traffic controllers use radar and radio to keep them apart | Each plane flies in its own painted lane in the sky | Pilots just look out of the window and swerve | Planes only fly one at a time over each country
exercise: How does your suitcase find the right plane? | A barcode on its tag is scanned as it rides hidden belts | A worker carries every bag and asks each pilot | The bag has a small engine that drives it | All bags go on every plane, just in case
exercise: What colour is a plane's black box? | Bright orange, so it can be found | Black, so it stays hidden from thieves | Silver, to match the metal of the plane | White, so it shows up when it lands in snow
exercise: What does the black box record? | Flight data and the sounds in the cockpit | Every film the passengers watched on the trip | The names and seats of all the passengers | The weather on the ground back at home
exercise: Runway 09 has a big number painted on it. Which way does it point? | Roughly east, to 90 degrees | It is the ninth runway built at the airport | It is for planes with 9 seats | Roughly west, to 270 degrees
exercise: Where does a plane keep most of its fuel? | Inside its wings | In a big tank under the passengers' seats | In the tail fin only | In barrels in the luggage hold
exercise: Why does a helicopter need a small tail rotor? | To stop the body spinning round the other way | To push the helicopter much faster forward, like a boat's propeller | To cool down the main engine | To blow away dust when it lands
exercise: Why must you never go near a helicopter until the crew says so? | Its blades spin so fast they are hard to see | It might suddenly fly away with you | Helicopters are always much too hot to touch after a flight, even the doors | The crew wants to keep it clean
exercise: Why does a hot-air balloon rise? | Hot air inside is lighter than the cold air outside | The burner shoots it straight up into the sky like a big rocket | It is pulled up by the clouds | Its basket is filled with a magic gas
exercise: How does a balloon pilot choose a direction? | By going up or down to find winds blowing other ways | By steering with a big wheel | By paddling with oars | By pointing the burner sideways so the hot flame pushes the balloon along
exercise: How does a drone tilt and turn? | Its rotors spin at different speeds | It flaps a pair of small wings | It is pulled by an invisible string | It leans the way the wind blows
exercise: Where must drones never be flown? | Near airports, crowds or power lines | Over any grass or fields at all, anywhere | In the daytime, when people can see them | Above a height of one metre from the ground
exercise: How does a passport gate know it is really you? | Its camera compares your face with the photo on your passport's chip | It reads your fingerprint through your shoes | It listens to your voice saying your name | It checks the colour of your passport cover
exercise: In which city did the first people fly in a hot-air balloon, in 1783? | Paris | London | New York | Rome
exercise: Count the rotors: 3 drones with 4 rotors each. How many rotors? | 12 | 7 | 10 | 16
test: «black box is black, duh, it's in the name» What is right? | No, it is bright orange so it can be found after an accident | Yes, it is black so it stays hidden | It is black on the outside, but bright orange and yellow inside the case | It changes colour after an accident
test: «at the scanner my cousin said there's a bomb in his bag as a joke» What will happen and why? | Staff must treat it as real, so it causes big trouble and delays | Nothing, everyone knows it's a joke | He gets through faster | The X-ray machine checks his voice and ignores it
test: Your little brother wants to ride the baggage carousel like a roundabout. What is right? | Never climb on it: it moves bags, not people, and can trap you | It is fine if he holds on tight | It is fine because it moves slowly and has soft rubber flaps all round the edge | Only if Mum rides with him
test: «why the heck do i need a whole control tower, can't pilots just look out the window» What is right? | Thousands of planes fly at once, and controllers use radar and radio to keep them apart | Pilots really do just look and swerve | Control towers only sell tickets | The tower is only for weather reports
test: In a cartoon, a balloon pilot turns a steering wheel and the balloon turns right. What is wrong? | Real balloons cannot steer; the pilot goes up or down to find a different wind | Nothing, balloons have rudders | Balloons steer with oars, not wheels | The pilot should blow on the balloon
test: «gonna fly my drone at the airport fence, wanna film the jets» What is the answer? | Never: it is illegal and can endanger planes | Fine, if the drone is small and you keep it low | Fine at night, when nobody can see it up there | Fine, as long as you stay under the clouds and the fence
test: What would happen if a helicopter lost its tail rotor? | The body would start spinning round under the main rotor | It would fly faster forward | Nothing at all, the tail rotor is only there for decoration and balance | It would rise straight up much faster
test: «drone hovers still with no wind?? magic» What is right? | Its rotors keep adjusting, and some use GPS to hold their place | It is hanging from an invisible wire | The air holds anything still once it is up high enough above the trees | Drones only fly when the wind holds them
test: Which runway points roughly south, to 180 degrees? | 18 | 1 | 9 | 36
test: Count them: at the helipad there are 2 helicopters, each with a main rotor and a tail rotor. How many rotors? | 4 | 2 | 3 | 6
test: «why do planes keep fuel in the wings, isn't that weird» Which is true? | Yes, most fuel is stored inside the wings | No, fuel is kept in the passengers' cabin | No, fuel is carried in a tank on the roof | No, planes carry no fuel, they run on batteries
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the device commonly designated the black box. | It comprises two robust recorders of flight data and cockpit sounds, coloured bright orange to facilitate their recovery | It is a single black container concealed in order to deter theft | It records solely the entertainment viewed by passengers | It is jettisoned by the pilot prior to every landing
exam: Identify the principal function of air traffic control. | The maintenance of separation between aircraft by means of radar and radio, together with the issuing of clearances to depart and land | The sale of tickets and the allocation of seats | The refuelling of aircraft upon the apron | The reporting of weather conditions to passengers exclusively
exam: Ascertain the means whereby the pilot of a hot-air balloon may alter the direction of travel. | By ascending or descending in order to encounter winds blowing in other directions | By the operation of a rudder affixed to the basket and turned by means of a wheel | By rowing with oars against the air | By directing the burner horizontally
exam: Whereas a runway bears the numeral 27, determine the approximate compass direction in which it is aligned. | Approximately 270 degrees, that is to say towards the west | Approximately 27 degrees, that is to say towards the north-east | It denotes the twenty-seventh runway constructed at the aerodrome | Approximately 90 degrees, that is to say towards the east
